# 
# But I still think 6.9 Mb is outrageous for a kernel size.  Would
# possibly stripping various modules BEFORE linking them into the kernal
# do the trick?  Does anyone have a reason why DEC kernels in particular
# seem to be so large?

ULTRIX v4.3 MIPS kernels are over 3 MB, and are around 4 MB for
large machines with lots of different devices.  The kernels are
so big because there are so many different things supported.

I'm not sure if the 6.9 MB for DEC OSF/1 has the debugging turned on..

It's not just DEC that makes large kernels.
My OSF/1 rel 1.1 system on a DECstation 3100 has a 1.9 MB kernel.
This kernel has nothing much statically built in.  If I add in
all of the dynamically loadable pieces, the kernel will be over 4 MB!
Inet comes in at around 169 KB, NFS at 260 KB, LVM at 211 KB, etc..
Also I have some "large" Unicode tables and Japanese input method
modules to make the total size over 4 MB.
So ULTRIX and OSF/1 are not much different in size.

Also DEC OSF/1 is 64 bits..
