Only for genvmunix.  The BINARY .o's still have the full -g3 debug information
so that crash dump analysis can be done.

|>	Our DEC 7000-610 kernel was a big 6150072 bytes with debugging
|>	and profiling. I just built a _stripped_ OSF/1 kernel without
|>	profiling or debugging and the result is a 2783232 byte vmunix.
|>	The 5000/240 sitting on my desk runs a stripped ULTRIX kernel
|>	that takes only 1660856 bytes.

ULTRIX and OSF/1 does not like stripped kernel.  (ld -x does not produce a
stripped kernel -- it producs a kernel without debug or local symbols but all
the global symbols are still for nlist to find).

|>	'makeoptions LDOPTS="-x"' in your kernel config file will build
|>	a stripped /vmunix by default. The tradeoff here is that any
|>	kernel built with this option is totally useless for debugging
|>	crash dumps. No symbol table no crash dump analysis, so use this
|>	option with _caution_.

As I said before, -x does not produce a stripped kernel (-s would but you
don't want to do that).
