In article <1od7p0$i34@harpo.wi.mit.edu>, lstein@genome.wi.mit.edu (Lincoln Stein) writes:
|>I'm using a DEC 3000 alpha running prerelease OSF1.  Can someone tell
|>me whether it's possible to designate an additional file for swap
|>space without repartitioning a disk?
|>
|>On a sun, it's easy to create a swap file using mkfile, and then
|>designate it as swap using swapon.  While OSF1 has swapon, it doesn't
|>seem to have an equivalent to mkfile.  I've tried creating a file of
|>0's and setting the sticky bit, but swapon complains that the "device
|>is not configured."

Find an empty disk partition (but not an "a" parition or you may wipe out your
disklabel) and do:

	swapon /dev/rzXp

where X is the disk number and p is the parition (a-h).  To get it automaticlly
mounted, add it to /etc/fstab thusly (I'm using rz0b as the second swap
parition):

/dev/rz0g                       swap1                   ufs sw          0 2
/dev/rz0b                       swap2                   ufs sw          0 2

Another alternative to consider to switch your OSF/1 system to lazy allocation
swap space instead of the default eager allocation.  To do this, remove the
link /sbin/swapdefault (which is a symbolic link to the swap parition block
device entry in /dev).
