>   as the host for a system that could run either OS/2
>   or AIX.  This would not be OSF/1, which is based on
>   the Mach 2.5 microkernel.

IBM's work might be based on OSF/1 MK. I have never read or heard
anything that would formally confirm that, however. Such things have
been demonstrated under Mach 3.0 before (i.e., simultaneously
executing DOS and BSD servers).

>   So far, only DEC has announced a commercial OSF/1.
>   Anybody know any different?

That depends on what you mean by commercial.

- IBM announced a native version of OSF/1 for their ES/9000
mainframes. Don't know how many takers on that, though.

- I believe Encore was going to ship OSF/1 on (some of?) their
products.

- I recall hearing Hitachi announcing an OSF/1 product for their
IBM-compatible mainframes also.
 
- KSR is (was) using OSF/1 MK on their supercomputer.

- Intel announced plans to run OSF/1 MK on their supercomputer.

For obvious reasons, none of the above have made a significant market
penetration. I'd be surprised if anyone surpasses DEC in volume in the
forseeable future. IBM's AIX/OS/2 stuff might be the only one with any
chance of shipping in volume.
  If anyone has any information that would add to or prove what I
wrote above wrong, I'd be thrilled to hear about it. 8-)

