Try it. Many programs compile out of the box, some need little
adjusting, some need patches you can find (mostly) in comp.unix.osf.osf1.

Here is a list of freely available software running on "my" Alpha:

# patch 	: first things first, NO problems
# tcsh-6.02	: need patch from gatekeeper.dec.com:/pub/DEC/Alpha/apps
# gcc-2.3.3	: needs a patch found in comp.unix.osf.osf1; no "gcc -g";
		  fails "make compare" 
# less		: no problems
# GNU-tar, find : vprintf seems to be broken; must #undef HAVE_VPRINTF
		  in Makefile after running ./Configure
# perl		: use 'dec-osf' instead of 'osf1', which is the default
		  from ./configure; needs little patch I found in
		  comp.unix.osf.osf1 
# zip and unzip : no problems
# gzip		: since version 1.0.6
# ghostscript   : compiles smoothly; creates "unaligned access" warnings for 
		  every(!) letter processed but works fine after a "uac p 0"

[I haven't heard of successful attempts to compile TeX and bash. They
 probably depend on 4-byte longs.]

Does anybody know of a place to collect Alpha-relevant information? If
