The online man page for "mountd" says

  When getting the signal SIGHUP, mountd rereads the exports file.

However, if I modify /etc/exports, and do a "kill -HUP pid" (where pid is
mountd's process ID), I notice that (1) the atime of /etc/exports doesn't
get updated, and (2) requests to mount the newly-exported filesystem fail
with an "access denied" message.  So it appears that either the man page
is wrong (and there's some other way to make mountd reread /etc/exports),
or mountd is buggy.

Short of "/sbin/init.d/nfs stop; /sbin/init.d/nfs start", how *does* one
cause /etc/exports to be reread after it's been modified?

